g_mc="""<h2>How to merge CSV and Excel files</h2><ol><li>Choose two or more CSV, TSV or Excel (XLSX, XLS) files. You can mix them.</li><li>Put the files in the order you want with the ↑ ↓ buttons or by dragging; rows are added in that order. For Excel files with several sheets, pick the sheet.</li><li>Choose how columns are matched, whether to add a <b>Source file</b> column, and the output format, then click <b>Merge files</b>.</li></ol>
<h2>How columns are matched</h2><ul><li><b>By header name</b> (default): the result has every column that appears in any file, in the order they first appear. A file without a column gets empty cells there, so data never slides into the wrong column. Names match exactly after removing spaces at the start and end; tick <b>Ignore upper/lower case and extra spaces</b> to treat "Email" and "email " as the same column. Two columns with the same name in one file are kept apart as "Name" and "Name (2)".</li><li><b>By position</b>: column 1 goes under column 1, and so on, using the header of the first file. Use it when the files have the same layout but different header text.</li></ul><p>The header is written once. Rows that have more or fewer cells than their header are counted and reported: missing cells stay empty and extra cells go into "Extra" columns.</p>
<h2>Encodings and delimiters</h2><p>UTF-8 (with or without BOM) and UTF-16 files, such as Excel's "Unicode Text", are detected automatically. Older files can be opened as Windows-1252, Windows-1258 (Vietnamese) or Shift_JIS (Japanese Excel); if a file is not valid UTF-8 the tool asks you to choose and shows a preview. Characters that cannot be decoded are counted and shown as �, never silently removed. Text is normalized to Unicode NFC, so Vietnamese names match across files. The delimiter (comma, semicolon, tab or pipe) is detected from the rows, so a semicolon file with decimal commas such as 1,5 is read correctly; you can also set it yourself.</p>
<h2>Output</h2><ul><li><b>CSV</b> is saved as UTF-8 with a BOM so accents open correctly in Excel. Excel in many European and Vietnamese settings expects semicolons, so you can choose the output delimiter.</li><li><b>XLSX</b> keeps values such as 0912345678, 00123, 16-digit account numbers, 1E5 or 04/11/2026 exactly as written (stored as text); only plain numbers are stored as numbers. Excel allows at most 1,048,576 rows per sheet.</li><li>Large files are processed in a background worker with a progress bar and a Cancel button, so the page stays responsive.</li></ul>
"""+FAQ2(("Are my files uploaded?","No. Files are read and merged inside your browser; nothing is sent to a server, and the tool keeps working offline once the page has loaded."),("Why does Excel change 0912345678 when I open the CSV?","Excel converts values that look like numbers when it opens a CSV. The merged CSV still contains 0912345678; choose XLSX output if you want Excel to show it unchanged."),("What does the formula protection do?","A cell that starts with = + - or @ can run as a formula when a CSV is opened in a spreadsheet (CSV injection). With the option on, such cells get a leading apostrophe; normal negative numbers like -1500 are not changed. XLSX output always stores them as plain text."),("Can I merge files with different columns?","Yes. With matching by header name every column is kept, and cells are left empty where a file has no value."),("What happens to dates and formulas in Excel files?","The displayed value is used: formulas become their results and dates are written as dates (for example 2026-11-04), not as serial numbers."))
add2("merge-csv","Spreadsheet tools","Merge CSV & Excel Files","Merge CSV & Excel Files Online – Combine Sheets, No Upload","Combine many CSV, TSV and Excel files into one: match columns by name or position, keep leading zeros, add a source-file column, export CSV or XLSX. Runs in your browser.",ui_mc,js_mc,g_mc)

